# apps/marketing — environment
#
# Copy to `.env.local` for development. On Vercel these are Project Settings →
# Environment Variables; the runbook is docs/deploy/marketing-vercel.md.
#
# Every variable here is read by code in this repo or by a dependency, and the
# runbook names the file and line for each. Nothing aspirational is listed: a
# variable nobody reads is a variable somebody will set and then trust.
#
# Two rules worth internalising before you touch anything below:
#
#   1. `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` is inlined at BUILD time, not read at runtime. Changing
#      one in the Vercel dashboard does nothing until you redeploy.
#   2. Every unset variable here has a deliberate, safe failure mode — except
#      the waitlist sink. Set DATABASE_URL (or WAITLIST_WEBHOOK_URL) or the
#      site collects nothing. Read its note.


# --- The waitlist ----------------------------------------------------------
# src/lib/waitlist-store.ts. TWO sinks, tried in this order:
#
#   1. DATABASE_URL         — a Neon Postgres branch. Preferred.
#   2. WAITLIST_WEBHOOK_URL — POST the JSON somewhere else.
#
# NEITHER SET IS NOT SAFE, and this is the only pair in this file that isn't.
# `saveSignup()` THROWS `WaitlistNotConfigured`, the server action logs a
# refusal, and the form shows its error state. That is the correct behaviour —
# a form reporting success while discarding the signup is the one outcome this
# must never produce — but it means the site collects zero signups while looking
# entirely healthy from the outside.
#
# SET ONE OF THEM BEFORE YOU SEND ANYONE TO THE SITE.


# --- Sink 1: Neon Postgres (preferred) -------------------------------------
# The pooled connection string from the Neon Console (Connect → the one with
# `-pooler` in the host). Read at runtime, so no redeploy is needed to change
# it — but DO create the table first, or every signup errors:
#
#   psql "$DATABASE_URL" -f apps/marketing/sql/001_waitlist_signups.sql
#
# or paste that file into the Neon Console's SQL Editor.
#
# Writes with ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING on (audience, contact), so a visitor who
# presses the button twice gets a silent success rather than a duplicate row,
# and their FIRST consent timestamp is preserved rather than overwritten.
#
# Treat it as a credential: it grants full read/write to that branch. Neon's
# dashboard can rotate it, which invalidates the old one immediately.
DATABASE_URL=


# --- Sink 2: a webhook (fallback) ------------------------------------------
# Only consulted when DATABASE_URL is unset.
#
# Use this instead of DATABASE_URL when the signup has to reach something
# somebody else runs — a CRM, Zapier, a Google Sheet. Setting both is not an
# error; Postgres simply wins and this is never consulted.
#
# The receiver gets one POST per signup, `content-type: application/json`, and
# must answer 2xx — any other status throws and the visitor sees the error. It
# is not retried: a duplicate signup is worse than a retryable message, because
# the visitor can press the button again and we cannot un-send an email.
#
# Body (the `Signup` type):
#   {
#     "audience":    "customer" | "restaurant" | "rider",
#     "contact":     "someone@example.com",   // normalised, lower-cased
#     "kind":        "email",                 // while O-03 is open; see below
#     "consentText": "I agree to receive one launch email from Halal Goes, …",
#     "consentedAt": "2026-09-21T14:03:11.427Z",
#     "context":     "hero" | "final" | "footer" | "sticky",
#     "utm":         { "utm_source": "…" }    // {} for a direct visit
#   }
#
# `consentText` and `consentedAt` ship on every signup on purpose: CASL wants
# the wording, not the fact. Whatever you point this at MUST STORE ALL THREE of
# audience, consentText and consentedAt — a receiver that keeps only the email
# address throws away the defence the field exists to provide.
WAITLIST_WEBHOOK_URL=


# --- SEO -------------------------------------------------------------------
# The canonical origin. src/lib/site.ts.
#
# Every canonical tag, Open Graph URL and sitemap entry is built from it, and
# `isProductionSite()` gates robots.txt on it.
#
# Leave it UNSET on preview deploys. The build then uses Vercel's own host for
# that deploy, canonicals stay self-referential, and robots.txt disallows the
# whole origin. Set it to production on a preview and every preview page
# canonicalises to production and invites crawling.
#
# Indexing needs three signals to agree — Vercel says production, this is a
# production build, and the resolved origin really is https://halalgoes.com —
# precisely so "forgot to set it" cannot read as production. That guard leans on
# VERCEL_ENV and VERCEL_URL existing, so Vercel's "Enable access to System
# Environment Variables" must stay ON. See the runbook, §6.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://halalgoes.com


# --- The launch countdown --------------------------------------------------
# src/lib/launch.ts. Specced in docs/planning/marketing-site-plan.md §9.
#
# UNSET (the default) renders NO countdown — the chip above the headline reads
# "Ontario · launching soon". Forgetting to set this therefore yields no
# countdown, never a wrong one.
#
# It deliberately does NOT fall back to a waitlist counter: a signup count is on
# the do-not-publish list in src/lib/claims.ts until it can be read live from
# the database, and an invented number on a page whose whole claim is honesty
# about numbers would be the worst place to start.
#
# A date in the past switches to the live state rather than counting negative.
# An unparseable value is treated as unset and warns outside production.
#
# RFC 3339, e.g. 2026-11-03T12:00:00-05:00
NEXT_PUBLIC_LAUNCH_AT=


# --- Analytics (Umami, self-hosted) ----------------------------------------
# src/components/Analytics.tsx.
#
# BOTH must be set or no tag is rendered at all — not a tag pointing at
# `undefined`. The tag ships inert (`type="text/plain"`) and only runs once
# someone consents to measurement in the Klaro banner, so the failure mode of a
# broken consent manager is "no analytics", never "analytics anyway".
#
# Production only. A preview reporting into production's numbers means your
# first month of data is your own deploys. (The tag pins `data-domains` to the
# resolved origin's host, so Umami would reject it anyway — but don't rely on
# the second line of defence for something a checkbox fixes.)
#
# analytics.halalgoes.com is a separate A record on the Contabo VPS, NOT a
# Vercel domain. Same apex is the point: it makes the tracker first-party.
# See deploy/umami/.
NEXT_PUBLIC_UMAMI_SRC=
NEXT_PUBLIC_UMAMI_WEBSITE_ID=


# --- CMS (Keystatic) -------------------------------------------------------
# keystatic.config.ts, src/app/keystatic/layout.tsx, and @keystatic/core itself.
#
# UNSET = local storage: the editor at /keystatic writes to the working tree and
# has no login. Right on a laptop, wrong on a public origin — so
# src/app/keystatic/layout.tsx 404s the route in production until GitHub mode is
# configured. Writing posts locally needs none of these.
#
# ALL FOUR are required together for GitHub mode. The 404 guard only checks the
# first two, so setting CLIENT_ID and CLIENT_SECRET while forgetting
# KEYSTATIC_SECRET gets you a reachable editor whose API route throws on every
# request. Set all four, or none.
#
# Create the GitHub App at https://github.com/settings/apps with callback URL
# <origin>/api/keystatic/github/oauth/callback, or let Keystatic create it for
# you from /keystatic/setup in dev — it writes these four into .env itself.
# Runbook §4.
KEYSTATIC_GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=
KEYSTATIC_GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Signs and encrypts the session cookie that carries the editor's GitHub token.
# MUST BE AT LEAST 32 CHARACTERS — Keystatic throws
# "KEYSTATIC_SECRET must be at least 32 characters long" below that, at request
# time rather than build time. Generate with:
#   openssl rand -hex 40
# Treat it as a credential: rotating it signs every editor out, which is also
# how you revoke a compromised session.
KEYSTATIC_SECRET=

# The GitHub App's slug, read by @keystatic/next in the browser bundle to build
# the "install the app" links. NEXT_PUBLIC_, so it is inlined at build time and
# needs a redeploy to change. Without it the editor loads but its setup and
# repo-not-found screens cannot link anywhere useful.
NEXT_PUBLIC_KEYSTATIC_GITHUB_APP_SLUG=
